WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.516 --> 00:00:10.476
Lindsay Levin: Good to see you both.

00:00:10.516 --> 00:00:12.716
So the book is called "Speed and Scale."

00:00:12.716 --> 00:00:15.516
But I want to focus on the subtitle.

00:00:15.516 --> 00:00:19.116
The subtitle being --
where have I gone with this? --

00:00:19.116 --> 00:00:21.916
"An action plan to solve
our climate crisis now."

00:00:21.916 --> 00:00:23.596
What's the plan, John?

00:00:23.996 --> 00:00:28.116
John Doerr: The plan
is to transform society.

00:00:28.996 --> 00:00:31.476
LL: And what do you mean
by that, transform society?

00:00:31.476 --> 00:00:32.676
(Laughter)

00:00:32.716 --> 00:00:35.036
JD: I thought you might ask that.

00:00:35.076 --> 00:00:36.996
There are six big objectives.

00:00:37.556 --> 00:00:39.596
We’re going to electrify transportation,

00:00:39.636 --> 00:00:42.636
which means stop using diesel
and gas for our vehicles.

00:00:43.156 --> 00:00:48.116
We're going to decarbonize the grid
with wind and solar and nuclear.

00:00:48.156 --> 00:00:52.716
Third, we're going to fix
our food systems.

00:00:52.756 --> 00:00:56.276
And that includes
eating less meat and dairy.

00:00:56.636 --> 00:00:58.516
Reducing food waste

00:00:58.556 --> 00:01:00.916
and improving our soil health.

00:01:00.956 --> 00:01:03.356
Fourth, we're going to protect nature.

00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:05.996
That's stopping deforestation.

00:01:07.236 --> 00:01:08.636
Protecting our oceans,

00:01:08.676 --> 00:01:11.196
protecting our peatlands, our grasslands.

00:01:11.796 --> 00:01:14.756
Fifth, we're going to
clean up our materials,

00:01:14.796 --> 00:01:17.676
how we make things like cement and steel.

00:01:18.196 --> 00:01:19.756
And then sixth,

00:01:19.756 --> 00:01:23.236
we're going to have to figure out
ways to remove the carbon that remains.

00:01:23.276 --> 00:01:28.956
That stubborn, residual effects
of emissions that cannot be eliminated.

00:01:29.276 --> 00:01:33.156
Every one of these six things
is a major challenge.

00:01:33.196 --> 00:01:35.076
We've got to attack them all at once.

00:01:35.116 --> 00:01:37.236
LL: And how do we do that on time, Ryan?

00:01:37.276 --> 00:01:38.916
How are we going to get this done?

00:01:38.916 --> 00:01:41.676
Ryan Panchadsaram: So we've got
to attack them all at once,

00:01:41.716 --> 00:01:43.156
but we've got to move quickly.

00:01:43.196 --> 00:01:45.036
And so the plan has four accelerants.

00:01:45.036 --> 00:01:47.796
Think of these as the levers
that we can pull on equally.

00:01:47.836 --> 00:01:49.796
We've got to win the politics and policy,

00:01:49.836 --> 00:01:53.356
so the commitments that are being made
actually have follow through.

00:01:53.916 --> 00:01:56.916
And then we've got to turn
movements into real action,

00:01:56.956 --> 00:01:58.276
at the ballot box

00:01:58.316 --> 00:02:00.396
as well as in the corporate boardrooms.

00:02:00.756 --> 00:02:02.276
And then we've got to innovate.

00:02:02.276 --> 00:02:05.596
Innovate to drive down the cost
of clean technologies,

00:02:05.596 --> 00:02:07.196
and then we have to invest.

00:02:07.196 --> 00:02:11.516
We have to invest in research,
in deployment, in philanthropy.

00:02:11.556 --> 00:02:14.156
We do all those things, Lindsay,
we get to move faster.

00:02:14.156 --> 00:02:17.276
LL: So that's the plan in a nutshell,
but what makes it different?

00:02:17.316 --> 00:02:20.676
JD: What's different
about the Speed and Scale plan

00:02:20.716 --> 00:02:24.636
is it's based on objectives
and key results.

00:02:24.676 --> 00:02:26.356
Or OKRs.

00:02:26.836 --> 00:02:28.436
If you're not familiar with them,

00:02:28.436 --> 00:02:33.436
what OKRs are is a proven system
to set goals for success

00:02:33.476 --> 00:02:37.476
that's been used by large
and small organizations alike.

00:02:37.516 --> 00:02:41.396
And the benefit of using them
is they help you focus,

00:02:41.436 --> 00:02:42.796
get alignment, commitment

00:02:42.836 --> 00:02:47.676
and track your progress over time
so that we get everything done.

00:02:48.756 --> 00:02:52.236
Objectives are what you want
to have accomplished;

00:02:52.276 --> 00:02:55.956
key results are how
I get that done in time.

00:02:56.396 --> 00:03:00.756
Really good key results
are concrete and measurable.

00:03:00.796 --> 00:03:06.116
And so they're what turn a set of goals
into a real action plan.

00:03:06.156 --> 00:03:07.916
LL: Can you give us an example, Ryan?

00:03:07.956 --> 00:03:09.196
RP: Yeah, of course.

00:03:09.196 --> 00:03:11.036
So let's pick on that first objective,

00:03:11.076 --> 00:03:14.476
to electrify transportation,
which cuts six gigatons.

00:03:14.516 --> 00:03:17.796
So every set of these objectives
have a handful of key results.

00:03:17.836 --> 00:03:20.116
And so for this first one, there are six.

00:03:20.116 --> 00:03:24.116
An example of one is
the price of electric vehicles

00:03:24.156 --> 00:03:25.636
have to be cheaper

00:03:25.676 --> 00:03:28.396
than the fossil-fuel equivalent by 2024.

00:03:28.956 --> 00:03:30.876
Or another one, by 2025,

00:03:30.916 --> 00:03:34.836
all new buses have to be electric,
all the new purchased ones.

00:03:34.836 --> 00:03:37.516
And so these key results tell us
if we're making progress

00:03:37.556 --> 00:03:39.236
and if we're getting there on time.

00:03:39.236 --> 00:03:41.796
And so if electric cars
are still expensive

00:03:41.836 --> 00:03:45.596
or we're still seeing
diesel buses sold after 2025,

00:03:45.636 --> 00:03:48.316
we know we're off track
and we have to course correct.

00:03:48.356 --> 00:03:51.436
LL: So what I hear you saying
is that we need to be accountable,

00:03:51.476 --> 00:03:52.956
we need to be super ambitious,

00:03:52.996 --> 00:03:56.396
we need to be very practical
because of the scale of change needed.

00:03:56.396 --> 00:04:00.356
Now, John, you have helped grow
some of the most successful companies

00:04:00.356 --> 00:04:01.556
in the world.

00:04:01.556 --> 00:04:04.716
And when I think about the conversations
that go on in boardrooms,

00:04:04.716 --> 00:04:06.076
I can't help but think

00:04:06.116 --> 00:04:08.756
that some of the leaders there
will be frankly daunted,

00:04:08.796 --> 00:04:11.276
maybe aghast, even horrified

00:04:11.276 --> 00:04:14.556
at the scale and speed
and breadth and depth

00:04:14.596 --> 00:04:16.836
of the transformation
that you're talking about.

00:04:16.876 --> 00:04:19.196
What is your message
to your business peers?

00:04:20.116 --> 00:04:22.436
JD: My message to them is simple.

00:04:23.036 --> 00:04:27.516
It's that climate change
has been underhyped,

00:04:27.516 --> 00:04:28.756
underhyped.

00:04:28.796 --> 00:04:31.316
We are underestimating

00:04:32.356 --> 00:04:34.796
the economic opportunity

00:04:34.796 --> 00:04:37.676
and the risk in this transition.

00:04:38.596 --> 00:04:40.076
The human cost,

00:04:40.116 --> 00:04:45.276
the economic toll that can come
if we don't seize this opportunity,

00:04:45.316 --> 00:04:48.676
which could create
25 million jobs, new jobs,

00:04:48.716 --> 00:04:50.716
in the next decade alone --

00:04:50.716 --> 00:04:52.516
or wreck our communities.

00:04:52.916 --> 00:04:54.916
I want to ask you, friends,

00:04:54.916 --> 00:04:58.516
how much more damage do we have to endure

00:04:58.516 --> 00:05:02.876
before we realize that it's cheaper
to save this planet than to ruin it?

00:05:03.476 --> 00:05:07.196
(Applause)

00:05:08.196 --> 00:05:11.236
LL: One of the things that people
often say about climate change

00:05:11.276 --> 00:05:13.996
is that we already have
all of the solutions that we need,

00:05:14.036 --> 00:05:17.396
and the real issue is that we’ve just
got to get on and implement them.

00:05:17.396 --> 00:05:19.316
And I believe and I read in the book

00:05:19.316 --> 00:05:21.356
that you're saying
that's not enough, Ryan,

00:05:21.396 --> 00:05:22.596
talk to us about that.

00:05:22.636 --> 00:05:25.836
Why do we need something more
than what we already have?

00:05:25.876 --> 00:05:29.156
RP: I think of it
as a “yes, and,” right?

00:05:29.196 --> 00:05:32.316
We have 85 percent
of the solutions that we need.

00:05:32.756 --> 00:05:36.796
Record lows of solar and wind prices
means deployments around the world,

00:05:36.836 --> 00:05:41.716
the dropping cost of lithium-ion batteries
means we're seeing more electric vehicles.

00:05:41.716 --> 00:05:45.116
But those solutions
alone won't get us to net-zero.

00:05:45.116 --> 00:05:50.996
And so we're going to have to both
deploy and invest in the now

00:05:50.996 --> 00:05:52.396
as well as invent the new.

00:05:52.436 --> 00:05:53.916
So we need the now and the new,

00:05:53.956 --> 00:05:57.036
we need to scale up what we have
as well as invest in the future.

00:05:57.076 --> 00:05:59.436
And there are two
pretty tangible examples, right,

00:05:59.436 --> 00:06:02.556
when you think of solar and wind
as it gets deployed,

00:06:02.596 --> 00:06:05.196
you can't turn that on and off
when you need it, right?

00:06:05.196 --> 00:06:07.836
So a grid needs to find a way
to fill its gaps.

00:06:07.876 --> 00:06:11.636
Hence, next-level battery technologies
or even safer nuclear.

00:06:11.676 --> 00:06:13.436
One of those could fill the gaps.

00:06:13.436 --> 00:06:15.716
Or think about how much we fly.

00:06:15.716 --> 00:06:18.396
Carbon-neutral fuels need to be developed,

00:06:18.436 --> 00:06:20.316
and the cost needs to be driven down.

00:06:20.356 --> 00:06:22.916
The goal of all of this
at the end of the day

00:06:22.956 --> 00:06:25.356
is to try to take these green premiums,

00:06:25.396 --> 00:06:29.116
and if they can become green discounts
we’ll see this technology everywhere.

00:06:29.156 --> 00:06:31.636
LL: One of the things in the book
is carbon removals,

00:06:31.676 --> 00:06:34.596
which you believe is imperative
to solving this problem.

00:06:34.596 --> 00:06:36.676
And when people think
about carbon removals,

00:06:36.716 --> 00:06:38.716
they get understandably suspicious.

00:06:38.756 --> 00:06:41.396
Because historically,
it's been an excuse for inaction.

00:06:41.396 --> 00:06:44.436
We can continue polluting,
and we’ll clean up later.

00:06:44.476 --> 00:06:46.196
You're telling us in your view

00:06:46.236 --> 00:06:48.956
that carbon removals
are an imperative piece of the plan.

00:06:48.956 --> 00:06:51.276
Can you describe why
and what you mean by that?

00:06:51.276 --> 00:06:53.716
RP: Of course. I mean,
people should be suspicious.

00:06:53.716 --> 00:06:55.756
Carbon removal needs to be the last piece.

00:06:55.756 --> 00:06:58.596
So as an organization,
if you're trying to get to net-zero,

00:06:58.596 --> 00:07:01.116
the first thing
you have to do is cut, right?

00:07:01.116 --> 00:07:04.116
Pick the alternative,
pick the electric alternative.

00:07:04.156 --> 00:07:05.916
Then you’ve got to be more efficient.

00:07:05.956 --> 00:07:08.036
So you've got to cut,
become more efficient,

00:07:08.036 --> 00:07:11.236
and then Lindsay, then people
can rely on carbon removal.

00:07:11.236 --> 00:07:15.356
But when you look at all the models
from IPCC or even our rough modeling,

00:07:15.396 --> 00:07:17.756
you're still going to have
10 gigatons left over.

00:07:17.756 --> 00:07:20.556
And so we've got to invest
in carbon-removal technologies

00:07:20.596 --> 00:07:23.396
that are both nature-based
as well as engineered,

00:07:23.396 --> 00:07:25.516
because we're going to
need it in the future.

00:07:25.556 --> 00:07:26.756
LL: Climate justice.

00:07:26.756 --> 00:07:28.356
Let's talk about climate justice.

00:07:28.356 --> 00:07:29.796
It's a big theme in the book.

00:07:29.796 --> 00:07:33.316
John, you are an affluent white American,

00:07:33.316 --> 00:07:35.276
white male American.

00:07:36.076 --> 00:07:37.876
Yay, exactly.

00:07:37.916 --> 00:07:40.036
Any more of those in the room? Yeah.

00:07:41.196 --> 00:07:43.076
Tell us, from your viewpoint,

00:07:43.116 --> 00:07:45.956
how do you think about this question
of climate justice?

00:07:46.596 --> 00:07:48.836
JD: You know, when you think about it,

00:07:48.836 --> 00:07:50.756
climate justice,

00:07:50.796 --> 00:07:55.316
climate change, amplifies inequities.

00:07:55.996 --> 00:07:57.756
Those who suffer the most

00:07:57.756 --> 00:08:00.556
have done the least to cause this problem.

00:08:00.956 --> 00:08:04.276
(Applause)

00:08:04.996 --> 00:08:07.196
And what that means is that the US,

00:08:07.236 --> 00:08:10.516
as the world's historic biggest emitter,

00:08:10.516 --> 00:08:12.996
must decarbonize first.

00:08:13.316 --> 00:08:15.276
We've got to do that for two reasons.

00:08:15.276 --> 00:08:17.756
To show the world that it's possible.

00:08:18.316 --> 00:08:21.076
And to drive the cost down
for everyone else.

00:08:22.196 --> 00:08:24.116
More broadly,

00:08:24.156 --> 00:08:26.716
the US, Europe and China

00:08:26.756 --> 00:08:30.036
have to step forward
and fund the transition --

00:08:30.036 --> 00:08:34.916
all of the costs -- for a transition
to a new clean economy.

00:08:35.436 --> 00:08:39.956
Third, as we stop using fossil fuels,

00:08:39.956 --> 00:08:41.556
some of our communities,

00:08:41.596 --> 00:08:44.476
their livelihoods
are going to be left behind.

00:08:44.516 --> 00:08:46.396
Those jobs are going to evaporate.

00:08:46.756 --> 00:08:51.476
And so we've got to guarantee
that the good-paying jobs

00:08:51.516 --> 00:08:54.956
of the new clean economy
are available to them.

00:08:55.716 --> 00:08:59.116
LL: I want to finish
by asking you about leadership.

00:08:59.156 --> 00:09:03.676
So you say that the book is written
for the leader inside us?

00:09:03.716 --> 00:09:05.036
What's your call to action?

00:09:05.036 --> 00:09:06.836
We have leaders sitting in the room,

00:09:06.836 --> 00:09:08.196
we have leaders listening.

00:09:08.196 --> 00:09:09.876
What's the message to leaders?

00:09:09.876 --> 00:09:11.836
JD: Well, first, let's be clear,

00:09:11.876 --> 00:09:16.116
individual actions
are needed and expected.

00:09:16.756 --> 00:09:19.196
But they are not going to get us
where we need to go

00:09:19.236 --> 00:09:22.076
in this, the decisive decade,

00:09:22.076 --> 00:09:26.516
when we have to cut emissions
in half by 2030.

00:09:27.236 --> 00:09:31.156
Only concerted global action
is going to get this job done.

00:09:31.156 --> 00:09:33.916
And so we need each of us

00:09:33.956 --> 00:09:36.276
to mobilize others into action.

00:09:36.316 --> 00:09:39.636
That's what I mean by the inner
leader inside each of us.

00:09:39.636 --> 00:09:43.916
And we can be inspired by the actions
and the stories in this book.

00:09:44.316 --> 00:09:46.516
Like parents and teachers in Maryland

00:09:46.516 --> 00:09:50.116
who switch all the school
buses to be electric.

00:09:50.156 --> 00:09:54.116
Like workers who are demanding
that their organizations,

00:09:54.116 --> 00:09:56.356
their companies and employers

00:09:56.396 --> 00:10:00.956
both commit and then meet
the net-zero commitments.

00:10:01.316 --> 00:10:02.516
Or the protesters

00:10:02.556 --> 00:10:07.876
who today are opposed
to this Campbell offshore oil development.

00:10:08.276 --> 00:10:09.476
Audience: Yes!

00:10:09.476 --> 00:10:12.516
(Applause)

00:10:12.876 --> 00:10:15.956
JD: In my experience and in this plan,

00:10:15.956 --> 00:10:18.876
when people strive
for extraordinary things --

00:10:18.916 --> 00:10:21.596
and not just strive,
but plan to get there --

00:10:21.596 --> 00:10:24.796
the results can surpass all expectations.

00:10:25.436 --> 00:10:27.556
I want to tell you, friends,

00:10:27.556 --> 00:10:30.156
we've got to pull together,
we've got to act together,

00:10:30.156 --> 00:10:31.996
we’ve got to act now.

00:10:31.996 --> 00:10:34.676
Because we are fast running out of time.

00:10:36.036 --> 00:10:39.076
LL: Ryan, a final thought
for leaders of your generation.

00:10:39.076 --> 00:10:42.156
When you think about your peers,

00:10:42.196 --> 00:10:43.396
are people ready,

00:10:43.436 --> 00:10:47.756
are you seeing a shift in terms
of people's capacity and willingness

00:10:47.756 --> 00:10:49.876
to step up and create
this different future?

00:10:49.916 --> 00:10:51.236
RP: Absolutely, absolutely.

00:10:51.236 --> 00:10:53.676
I think this is, like, a time

00:10:53.716 --> 00:10:56.756
for intergenerational teaming up
on these things.

00:10:56.756 --> 00:10:57.996
I think one of the things

00:10:58.036 --> 00:11:00.436
that in doing the research
for the book we found is

00:11:00.436 --> 00:11:03.756
the leverage points
don't take millions of people.

00:11:03.756 --> 00:11:06.996
The leverage points just take five
or 10 people coming together

00:11:07.036 --> 00:11:10.156
and saying, "This policy
shouldn't happen,"

00:11:10.196 --> 00:11:11.996
or, "This research needs to be done

00:11:12.036 --> 00:11:14.596
to show why we shouldn't
go down this path,"

00:11:14.596 --> 00:11:16.316
or in the world that we both are in,

00:11:16.356 --> 00:11:18.836
just three people coming together
to start a company.

00:11:18.876 --> 00:11:22.276
So I think our generation
is jumping full into this, Lindsay.

00:11:22.316 --> 00:11:23.716
We can't wait to work with --

00:11:23.716 --> 00:11:25.156
LL: Brilliant. We wish you --

00:11:25.796 --> 00:11:28.676
JD: Let's do this with speed and scale.

00:11:28.716 --> 00:11:29.876
LL: Speed and scale.

00:11:29.916 --> 00:11:32.116
And we wish you
the very best with the plan.

00:11:32.156 --> 00:11:33.796
Thank you, thank you.

00:11:33.836 --> 00:11:36.236
(Applause)

